So that if you look at the first episode which was recorded,

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:10.950
All I had was a painted back cloth, which only required, let's say, 20 man-hours to execute rather than the 100 man-hours or 200 man-hours that was actually required to construct that left-hand side of the set.

00:29:11.180 --> 00:29:12.900
It gave them more time to build the scenery.

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:20.820
There were a lot of components. Many of the bits were actually boxed because some of them were quite fragile.

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:28.960
So it would all arrive in like an Ikea building set, and they would put the walls up, and then they'd add the screen in.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.820
Then they'd have the lights in the back of the screen, which were designed by visual effects.

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:37.440
Then the individual chairs and the seats and the control panel would all be unpacked.

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:40.980
I mean, they were actually packed very well, and they didn't actually get much damage.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.540
So they would all be unpacked and added.

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:47.770
And the set took probably about a day and a bit,

00:29:48.050 --> 00:29:49.630
because, of course, they were not only doing Liberate,

00:29:49.670 --> 00:29:51.210
they were doing maybe five other sets as well.

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:54.050
So the set would be an entire day set.

00:29:54.050 --> 00:29:58.370
It would be an overnight beforehand, a day set, and then an overnight finish.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:00.000
So you come in the next day,

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.200
and the set ideally would be finished.

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:05.580
If you look at the set, a lot of the elements in it are hexagonal.

00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:11.180
We wanted something that would just carry that vision right the way through

00:30:11.180 --> 00:30:19.090
and it meant that when we got to other parts of the ship

00:30:19.090 --> 00:30:22.590
we could always reuse the same elements to get from A to B.

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:26.590
We only had two corridors and two doors, maybe had three doors,

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:29.730
but you had to be running up and down the same corridor and bursting through...

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:40.430
same doors but from a different angle you never quite knew where you were coming from where you

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:48.590
were going but it was a very expensive set for the time i mean we were told that we were in the most

00:30:48.590 --> 00:30:55.360
expensive set that the bbc had ever had ever made and it looked great on telly it just when you're

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:57.200
in the studio it looks slightly different

00:31:00.540 --> 00:31:05.180
Seats on the Liberator, I think they were partially made, partially bought, so it was

00:31:05.180 --> 00:31:11.220
an adapted design, as far as I remember, and they were two shades of grey, light and charcoal

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:16.930
grey and silver grey, and I luckily never had to sit on them. The artists, I gather,

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:20.690
weren't that happy with them. They felt it was like getting on a bicycle.

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:25.650
I did have a bicycle seat, which has played havoc with me ever since, prostate problems,

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:30.000
but yes, it had a ridge in it, and I was sort of sat like that. The seats were

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:34.700
slightly different in that they were designed so that you stood and lent against them we couldn't

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:41.420
actually sit down you had to lean your bum against it basically check compensate for orbital drift

00:31:41.420 --> 00:31:55.420
and hold i had a a very sort of smart console with my forward boosters three two one the

00:31:55.420 --> 00:32:00.000
controls were based on the arms of anglepoy's lamps which looked

00:32:00.410 --> 00:32:03.260
Wonderful, I thought, first time round.

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:05.310
They looked good.

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:07.310
The whole set looked wonderful

00:32:07.310 --> 00:32:09.130
and had tremendous visual appeal

00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:13.780
to production, who liked it, and to the audience.

00:32:16.330 --> 00:32:18.720
Time went by.

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:21.440
Series sets would sometimes be refurbished

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:23.680
because, obviously, the set's going in and out every week.

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:24.860
It got a lot of knockings.

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:29.380
Sets were...

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.040
were left in the ring road week on, week out,

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:36.760
and then hauled back in.

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:39.280
And by the time they'd been pulled around

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:41.900
by half a dozen teddies and little trucks,

00:32:42.780 --> 00:32:44.340
they got smashed to pieces.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:46.920
What about the automatic repair system?

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:47.800
Oh, it'll do the job.

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:50.230
You would arrange for a partial refurbish

00:32:50.230 --> 00:32:51.490
to be done as the set was going up.

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:58.740
It's fantastic.

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:02.960
Repairs to the primary power channel are complete.

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:08.120
I approached it like a kind of outing, like a sort of fun outing,

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:10.380
and I was really going to enjoy this. It was going to be great fun.

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:14.120
There wasn't enough money, and it could well be that,

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:18.120
because there wasn't enough money, invention was the mother of necessity.

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:24.610
So finding yourself with maybe two or three very large sets to do,

00:33:24.830 --> 00:33:29.120
which might, in terms of actors, there might be 20 or 30 extras perhaps,

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:30.000
So the set is

00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:36.410
therefore defined by a certain size to get the actors in, to get cameras, lights and so on.

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:40.710
But nevertheless, there wasn't actually a huge amount of money, so I tended to resort

00:33:40.710 --> 00:33:48.110
to doing part-set, part-drape, part-set, part-drape. So I would make the good bits and spend some

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:52.410
money on them, and then the gaps would be drapes or slash curtains.

00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:54.730
Look at these coloured ribbons!

00:33:54.730 --> 00:33:55.830
Perhaps they were having a party.

00:33:55.830 --> 00:34:00.000
I did tend to use a lot of drapes, because drapes were much cheaper. You could actually

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:03.540
have an area of drapes for a lot less money than you can have an area of set.

00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:16.250
The script gambit arrived on my desk and again, like many of the scripts, which are a very

00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:20.080
good script, it did ask for more than actually we had money for.

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:26.350
So again, it was an occasion where a lot of drapes and part sets were necessary.

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:30.000
Friends and patrons, your attention!

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:41.080
please I have an important announcement I had seen a circus tent go up driving home from because I

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:45.890
live near Windsor and Chipperfields winter quarters used to be near where I lived and they

00:34:45.890 --> 00:34:52.220
were putting up part of a tent and when it they got the sides up and then the the part the the

00:34:52.220 --> 00:34:59.440
upper part which would then make a gable was hanging into the area and then went up on a

00:34:59.440 --> 00:35:00.000
hoist,

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.650
which was outside, and I thought, well, we could actually,

00:35:03.260 --> 00:35:05.700
that might well be right for Gambit.

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:07.320
So I did a quick drawing and made,

00:35:07.420 --> 00:35:09.640
we often made small models of what it might be like.

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:14.660
Went along a production meeting and suggested this, like a tent,

00:35:14.980 --> 00:35:17.300
only it couldn't be a whole tent because there wasn't enough money.

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:21.070
And I just made strips of material to represent the walls.

00:35:21.510 --> 00:35:24.890
So they'd come up to about a metre and a half, perhaps two metres,

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:28.910
and then the top part of the tent would actually dip into the set

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:30.000
and then go up out of shot.

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:36.130
and to reveal the artist who would then become our master of ceremonies.

00:35:36.130 --> 00:35:41.700
The material which surrounded her was a material called Slash Curtain, used very much on top

00:35:41.700 --> 00:35:45.010
of the pops in the 60s and 70s.

00:35:45.010 --> 00:35:50.590
I remember picking up the script and saying to the producer, you know, are we approaching

00:35:50.590 --> 00:35:55.300
this very much as a piece of theatre rather than a piece of high-tech telly?

00:35:55.300 --> 00:35:59.060
The high-tech telly we're doing anyway, you know, we know all about that, but really what

00:35:59.060 --> 00:36:00.000
we're doing is circus.

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:05.220
theatre and we should treat it in that way and that's the way it was played it was played almost

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:13.390
to the gallery. Vila this gallant sporting gentleman has agreed to end the evening with

00:36:13.390 --> 00:36:23.110
one final wager. It was very much a theatrical thing it was quite a large set and again there

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:28.630
wasn't a huge amount of money it was a sort of Prince Regent a kind of forerunner to a Blackadder

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:30.000
character at one really.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:35.610
Now princely stripes very much a stripey set up silver makeup in fact

00:36:35.610 --> 00:36:43.610
Which was also used during the the period of Prince Regent the early 1800s my designer tells me that it is patterned upon the

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:52.330
Attire of someone called the Prince Regent. Oh, what a pity that everybody doesn't enter into the spirit of carnival of

00:36:53.050 --> 00:36:57.640
Mardi Gras as wholeheartedly as you and I

00:36:58.420 --> 00:37:00.000
It was all very camp

00:37:00.350 --> 00:37:02.070
but enormously enjoyable.

00:37:02.070 --> 00:37:03.110
- What's going on?

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:04.230
What am I doing here?

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:06.830
- Healer, the flute offers you the white pieces

00:37:06.830 --> 00:37:09.680
and the advantage of the first move.

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:11.240
- That drink, I've been tricked.

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:13.560
- It was probably an episode that we laughed more on

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:16.560
and goodness knows we spent our time on Blake laughing a lot.

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:22.600
It inevitably happened that if you were doing Blake 7,

00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:25.040
you might well do all the filming

00:37:25.040 --> 00:37:28.370
for maybe three or four episodes,

00:37:28.370 --> 00:37:30.000
whilst the designer was actually

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.320
doing the set or maybe two designers were getting their sets ready for an

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:39.300
episode that might not be for a few months so we would I would maybe go out

00:37:39.300 --> 00:37:43.810
and do the film location work recce locations find them and so on build the

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:47.350
sets take them out set them up while somebody else was doing their own thing

00:37:47.350 --> 00:37:55.960
in the studio we were always in clay pits it was always February even in

00:37:55.960 --> 00:37:59.480
August it was always February it was always cold

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:08.850
and there were always explosions and there was one terrible location at a pit

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:13.610
we was at a large door that's sliding backwards and forwards and the artist ran them out

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:25.400
continuously all day. I was in a Range Rover, a very posh looking Range Rover

00:38:25.400 --> 00:38:30.000
so my car was actually very close to where the camera was because I could drive there

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:36.320
no i think not many other people had a four-wheel drive at that point so i drove the car quite close

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:42.160
and i was sitting in the driving seat writing the prop list for my next location so i was sitting in

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:47.050
the car and along comes david maloney knocks on the window you know usual go away leave me alone

00:38:47.050 --> 00:38:52.170
this is the producer so i wound up and said what do you want he said could i come in he was blue

00:38:53.290 --> 00:38:58.010
i said well yeah but don't make a mess so he got in the back immediately took over the back of the car

00:38:58.010 --> 00:39:00.000
it was his office for the rest of the shoot

00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:03.760
And I said, David, it's so cold out there.

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:06.480
You know, I can barely, I've got to go out in about five minutes and do something.

00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:07.580
I can barely go out.

00:39:08.060 --> 00:39:10.440
He said, yes, it's like Dunkirk, isn't it?

00:39:12.630 --> 00:39:21.740
On Harvest of Kairos, we had a length of fence, which I think then appeared in several other episodes.

00:39:23.840 --> 00:39:24.900
Obviously, someone liked the fence.

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:26.460
And someone had to climb over it.

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:30.000
Stuart Fell, the stuntman, climbed over and fell off and that.

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:37.790
kind of thing they're getting into this very very tiny set and we needed them to walk up

00:39:37.790 --> 00:39:44.540
into shot away from camera and then down into the module and we built the module and other

00:39:44.540 --> 00:39:47.800
bits but there was nothing else left i mean it was a case of bring something from home

00:39:47.800 --> 00:39:53.970
and see what we can do so we we put the camera along a piece of rostro along which we put

00:39:53.970 --> 00:40:00.000
i think some some uh what you call fablon it's in the bbc terms it's called martak so we martak

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:05.800
top of a piece of rostra we borrowed the ladder from another set and the chippy lashed it to the

00:40:05.800 --> 00:40:12.400
rostra and then because there was nothing else no money um i just got some empty cylinders which

00:40:12.400 --> 00:40:17.480
were i think dark green with yellow tops and i just put a line of them each side of the camera

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:29.250
and as the artist walked up into the frame then he just walked away and that was the set on a later

00:40:29.250 --> 00:40:30.000
sequence

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:32.120
we had a large spider.

00:40:33.160 --> 00:40:38.200
It must have been at least a couple of metres in diameter.

00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:39.520
It was quite large, quite low.

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:46.520
And the special effects team were very proud of this spider.

00:40:47.420 --> 00:40:48.620
They'd worked really hard on it.

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:53.210
And Jim Francis and the other visual effects designer,

00:40:53.570 --> 00:40:57.320
they were sort of all over this large red thing

00:40:57.320 --> 00:41:00.000
that was about as frightening as the light stage.

00:41:01.820 --> 00:41:06.450
thinks he means you and it was really unfortunate the instant they trundled

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:11.540
him out the rest of the crew fell about laughing it was very very funny I mean

00:41:11.540 --> 00:41:14.680
none of us dare laugh because of course you wouldn't laugh at another designer's

00:41:14.680 --> 00:41:19.090
work but it wasn't really very frightening you really didn't have the

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:27.570
dramatic impact he should have had we were doing a sequence again it was all

00:41:27.570 --> 00:41:30.000
the theatrical so it lent itself to a bit at the ceiling

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:30.940
and we had a lot of drapes.

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:35.060
On that episode, we were doing with Fiona Cummings, the director.

00:41:35.380 --> 00:41:37.660
Fiona wanted a very, very wide shot,

00:41:37.900 --> 00:41:39.380
which meant that we'd have to build a set

00:41:39.380 --> 00:41:40.500
maybe three or four metres high,

00:41:40.660 --> 00:41:42.080
which was impractical, even for Ealing.

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:45.990
So we built the set as high as we could in terms of money

00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:47.350
and in terms of practicality.

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:51.250
So what we did to create the ceiling was use a glass shot.

00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:55.880
Nowadays, of course, you'd do it on post-production on computer.

00:41:56.380 --> 00:41:58.410
At that point, of course, it wasn't that advanced.

00:41:58.550 --> 00:42:00.000
So if you wanted to put a ceiling

00:42:00.120 --> 00:42:02.200
onto a set where you couldn't have a ceiling

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:04.560
because, of course, a lot of it was top-lit.

00:42:06.020 --> 00:42:08.800
You would pre-design it, of course.

00:42:09.300 --> 00:42:10.520
The artist would come paint on glass,

00:42:10.600 --> 00:42:11.680
so you'd have a locked-off camera,

00:42:12.080 --> 00:42:14.160
he'd paint the set, paint the ceiling on,

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:16.900
and then you would cut to that shot as and when you wished.

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:26.390
It was late in the day.

00:42:26.450 --> 00:42:27.900
It must have been six or seven in the evening.

00:42:28.180 --> 00:42:29.940
My assistant, Joe Page, and I had been there

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.940
all day, dressing most of the other sets.

00:42:32.940 --> 00:42:36.020
We finally got onto this, the black and white set,

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:39.230
and it was almost a complete circle.

00:42:39.230 --> 00:42:42.040
And I was up the ladder,

00:42:42.040 --> 00:42:43.720
finishing off some bits and pieces.

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:46.700
Julie went out to buy some coffee in one of the tea bars,

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:48.860
and I heard the door go.

00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:52.710
In a sound stage, when the door goes,

00:42:52.710 --> 00:42:56.560
it's unmistakable, it's a huge bang noise.

00:42:56.560 --> 00:43:00.000
And it's a wooden floor, as Ealing was, and most films

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:04.440
studios are. So I heard the clatter of feet across the floor and I thought Julie would come

00:43:04.440 --> 00:43:10.720
round the set and I'd look back and see her. Well, she wasn't there. So I heard the door bang again

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:15.860
and feet come across the floor and I said, then she had the coffee and I said, Julie, did you just

00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:20.590
come in a moment ago? And she said, no, this is the first time I've come in. And at that precise

00:43:20.590 --> 00:43:27.760
moment, I looked up to my left and the gantry, which was the means by which the electricians

00:43:27.760 --> 00:43:30.000
has got from one side of the studio to the other,

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.380
the ground and they hung lights from it and so on the gantry was just above my

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:43.390
my left eye and a lady in a long gray blue gray coat walked from as it were

00:43:43.390 --> 00:43:47.110
camera right to camera left she walked over the gantry and then just appeared

00:43:47.110 --> 00:43:59.030
disappeared in the blackness

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:05.840
There was a slight smell of perfume, a very, very slight trace of perfume, and it wasn't

00:44:05.840 --> 00:44:08.540
Jilly because she was a long way away from me.

00:44:08.540 --> 00:44:10.380
And I said to her, "Did you see that?"

00:44:10.380 --> 00:44:12.980
And she was doing the coffee, and she said, "What?"

00:44:12.980 --> 00:44:14.980
And I said, "The lady on the gantry."

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:19.230
She said, "Ken, we're alone in the studio, everyone else went hours ago, and we should

00:44:19.230 --> 00:44:20.870
have gone too, by the way.

00:44:20.870 --> 00:44:21.870
We're alone.

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:22.870
There's nobody else here."

00:44:22.870 --> 00:44:25.410
And I said, "It was the alien ghost."

00:44:35.560 --> 00:44:38.700
Rumours of Death shared with Paul Munting, the other designer.

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:42.790
Paul did studio sets, I did location and location sets.

00:44:42.790 --> 00:44:46.950
Well, Rumours of Death, the house that was used was a private property

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:51.130
and they were very keen that it was never revealed where the location was.

00:44:51.130 --> 00:44:54.810
In fact, I don't think I know to this day where the location was.

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:57.690
I just went along on the coach with everybody else.

00:44:57.690 --> 00:45:00.000
Shot at Cornbury Park, which is

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:03.140
Sort of the back gate of Blenheim Palace, so Blenheim was their neighbour.

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:06.120
Very beautiful country house owned by Lord Rotherwick.

00:45:07.620 --> 00:45:11.160
And we were there sort of midweek to the weekend.

00:45:11.380 --> 00:45:18.240
So Friday night, I had to clear the hall and one or two other areas because they had a shoot coming.

00:45:18.740 --> 00:45:22.960
We, as you appreciate, were the BBC shoot, and what he had coming was a pheasant shoot.

00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:30.000
So Friday night, we did a big clean-up and tidied our things away into a side room.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:31.800
room, come Saturday

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:33.600
morning, very crack of dawn

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:35.940
we all arrive outside the house

00:45:35.940 --> 00:45:37.920
and the shoot, the fashion shoot

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:40.100
also arrive outside the house, so there's a great

00:45:40.100 --> 00:45:41.450
car park jam

00:45:41.450 --> 00:45:43.770
and everyone gets out of their car

00:45:43.770 --> 00:45:45.550
and starts talking, oh who are you, who are you, I'm

00:45:45.550 --> 00:45:47.710
so sorry, I'm BBC, I'm Marcus

00:45:47.710 --> 00:45:49.530
of Tavistock, and he was there with his wife

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:51.810
and oh gosh, that wonderful car

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:53.690
that kind of thing, so we're all asking what

00:45:53.690 --> 00:45:55.690
each other did, and the conversation eventually a loud

00:45:55.690 --> 00:45:57.900
whistle went, and production manager

00:45:57.900 --> 00:46:00.000
standing on a small wall, shouts with

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:04.480
the loud hailer, would everyone who is here to shoot Blake 7, please stand to the left?

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:09.680
